<%# "THIS PERIOD" — ONE BLOCK PER CATEGORY, ONE ROW PER RULE (two-shapes spec §3).
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    ** THE RULING THIS SECTION IS BUILT ON (Henry, 2026-09-05): "almost all categories will have
    multiple rules, so we need to base the UI knowing this." ** It was a flat list of category rows
    with the second and third rule indented under the first, which reads as a category with
    footnotes. A category is a BLOCK now: a header that says how many rules it carries and what they
    claim between them, and a row per rule that says what shape it is, what it has, and when.

    ** THE ORDER IS `#give_way_order` GROUPED BACK, AND IT IS THE ONLY SORT ON THE SCREEN. ** A
    block sits where its first-giving-way rule sits, and its rows sit where each of theirs does. The
    strip above walks THESE blocks (`HomePresenter#trouble_lines` is `#category_blocks` flattened
    and filtered, fix wave — MED-1), so the two panels cannot rank one category two ways: they are
    one list, read twice. See `ClaimRows#give_way_order` for what the key is.

    ** EVERY FIGURE IS A CLAIM, off the page's ONE `ClaimLedger`. ** Nothing is allocated into a
    category (computed-claims §5); each row comes off one `ClaimCalculator` and says the sentence its
    shape gives it:

      RATE      usage · a period          $310.00 of $400.00     resets Oct 1
      DATED     bill · every 12 months    $26.50 of $26.50       Sep 17 · ready
      DATED     choice · $5,000.00 by Jun 1, 2027   $283.33 of $5,000.00   Apr 2 · +$41.67
      NO RULE   spent $32.00              — the fact, no bar, no pressure

    THE WORDS ARE THE USER'S: spent, of, claimed, ready, short, overdue. "Set aside", "goal", "fund"
    and "builds up" are not here — nothing moves and nothing holds money.

    AT 375 (§3): one column, and the rows drop their bars. The bar is the least of what a row says —
    the figure beside it is the same fact in numbers — and it is the first thing to cost more width
    than it earns. %>
<section data-this-period>
  <%# THE HEADING IS THE SECTION'S OWN ARITHMETIC AND NOT THE CALENDAR'S. "N days left" used to live
      here as well as under the hero's bar, printed by two panels that could disagree; the period
      belongs to the runway now (`_runway.html.erb`) and this line says what the section is about.
      The claimed total is `#total_claims` — the SAME figure the money tile subtracts from checking,
      and it is Σ over these blocks by construction. %>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-3 gap-y-1 pb-2">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900" data-this-period-heading>
      This period ·
      <%= pluralize(presenter.category_blocks.size, "category") %> ·
      <%= pluralize(presenter.category_blocks.sum(&:rule_count), "rule") %>
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 tabular-nums" data-this-period-claimed>
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_claims) %> claimed
    </p>
  </div>

  <%# TWO COLUMNS FROM 1024px, ONE BELOW (§3). `items-start` so a two-rule block beside a five-rule
      one keeps its own height instead of stretching to match. %>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-start">
    <% presenter.category_blocks.each do |block| %>
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded overflow-hidden" data-category-block="<%= block.name %>">
        <%# THE HEADER TINTS WHEN ANY ROW IS OVER, SHORT OR OVERDUE (§3) — `CategoryBlock#trouble?`,
            which is deliberately WIDER than the trouble strip's own two triggers. See that method:
            a bill due in nine days with a third of its money missing is not yet a thing that needs a
            human, and it is exactly what a header tint is for. %>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-3 gap-y-1 px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 <%= block.trouble? ? "bg-status-danger-light" : "bg-gray-50" %>"
             data-block-header>
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= block.name %></h4>
            <span class="text-xs text-gray-500"><%= pluralize(block.rule_count, "rule") %></span>
          </div>
          <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 tabular-nums" data-block-claimed>
            <%= number_to_currency(block.claimed) %> claimed
          </span>
        </div>

        <% block.rows.each do |row| %>
          <%# `data-rule-row` IS NAMED BY THE LANE, NOT BY THE CATEGORY: a block may carry two rules
              and both would answer to the category's name. `ClaimLine#name` is the item's or
              "Whole category". %>
          <div class="flex gap-3 px-4 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0"
               data-rule-row="<%= row.name %>">
            <%# THE STRIPE IS THE RULE'S TYPE (rules-own-the-budget §3), which is a fact about the
                RULE and not about its category: a category may carry a bill beside a choice, and
                they give way at opposite ends of the walk. `aria-hidden`, because the same fact is
                the first word of the shape clause below — a screen reader would otherwise meet it
                twice. %>
            <span class="w-1 shrink-0 rounded <%= stripe_fill(row) %>" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-3">
                <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 truncate"><%= row.name %></p>
                <%# THE FIGURE (§3.4), red when the spending has passed what the rule had. `over?` is
                    the pre-clamp reader and the only one that can tell "spent it exactly" from
                    "spent more than there was". %>
                <p class="text-sm tabular-nums <%= row.over? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>"
                   data-rule-figure>
                  <%= figure_words(row) %>
                </p>
              </div>

              <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs <%= type_text_class(row) %>" data-rule-shape><%= shape_words(row) %></p>

              <%# THE BAR, AND IT IS THE ONE THING 375px DROPS (§3). The percent rides on the TRACK:
                  a 0% fill has no width, so Capybara cannot see it, and a hook there would be
                  unassertable on exactly the row a bar most needs pinning — the one nobody has spent
                  from. Green when it has arrived, red when it is over or short, olive while it is
                  still filling. %>
              <% if row.bar? %>
                <div class="mt-1.5 hidden sm:block h-1.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden"
                     role="progressbar"
                     aria-valuenow="<%= row.percent %>"
                     aria-valuemin="0"
                     aria-valuemax="100"
                     aria-label="<%= block.name %> · <%= row.name %>"
                     data-rule-bar="<%= row.percent %>"
                     data-rule-bar-state="<%= row.bar_state %>">
                  <div class="h-1.5 rounded-full <%= bar_fill(row) %>"
                       style="width: <%= row.percent %>%;"
                       data-rule-fill></div>
                </div>
              <% end %>

              <%# WHEN: one clause, and which one is `HomeHelper#when_words`. A rate rule in a period
                  with no boundary declared has nothing to say and renders no element at all. %>
              <% if when_clause = when_words(row) %>
                <p class="mt-1 text-xs <%= row.trouble? || row.short? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-500" %>"
                   data-rule-when>
                  <%= when_clause %>
                </p>
              <% end %>
            </div>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# A CATEGORY NO RULE CLAIMS, WITH SPENDING THIS PERIOD (answers-first §4): the fact, no bar, no
        pressure. Nothing claims these receipts, so a denominator would be inventing the pressure
        rather than reporting it. Zero-spend ones are absent by construction — the Categories page
        remains the full index. %>
    <% presenter.unbudgeted_rows.each do |row| %>
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded px-4 py-3 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3"
           data-unbudgeted-row="<%= row.category.name %>">
        <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900"><%= row.category.name %></span>
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 tabular-nums">spent <%= number_to_currency(row.spent) %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# Said plainly rather than left as an empty card: a blank panel on a money screen reads as
      something that failed to load. The door is the Budget page, which is where a rule — the thing
      that gives a category a claim — is written. %>
  <% if presenter.category_blocks.empty? && presenter.unbudgeted_rows.empty? %>
    <p class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded px-4 py-3 text-sm text-gray-500">
      Nothing is budgeted yet, and nothing has been spent this period.
      <%= link_to "Give a category a rule on the Budget page", budget_page_path, class: "text-status-info hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</section>
